1
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:08,920
Glasses and robots will watch for hours, and we'll ask them about what they saw.

2
00:00:09,070 --> 00:00:14,333
But for a vision-language model, one hour of video is six and a half gigabytes of tokens.

3
00:00:14,483 --> 00:00:18,952
Most memories cope by forgetting: they keep some frames in full and drop the rest.

4
00:00:19,102 --> 00:00:21,762
Memryoshka stores each token as a nested code,

5
00:00:21,612 --> 00:00:25,637
a shorter code inside a longer one, just like Russian Matryoshka dolls.

6
00:00:25,787 --> 00:00:30,361
As memory fills, one price per bit decides what to shorten and what to drop.

7
00:00:32,787 --> 00:00:36,378
Here, forty-eight minutes of film stream into seven megabytes.

8
00:00:36,228 --> 00:00:39,709
Frames from start to finish stay in memory, at lower detail.

9
00:00:39,559 --> 00:00:42,595
At full precision, the same memory would hold three.

10
00:00:44,287 --> 00:00:47,763
On six benchmarks it matches a memory that keeps everything,

11
00:00:47,613 --> 00:00:54,026
with about a thousand times less storage on hour-long videos, and beats existing memories by six to eight points.

12
00:00:54,176 --> 00:00:57,573
It also works for robots, and for audio and video together.

13
00:00:57,723 --> 00:00:59,293
No training needed.

14
00:00:59,143 --> 00:01:01,610
Memryoshka: fade, don't forget.
